<h1>Auto-Research</h1>
<p class="subtitle">A network-assisted source-gathering path, not automatic knowledge creation.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Off by default.</strong> Set <code>autoResearch: true</code> in <code>config.json</code> or <code>MCP_LIBRARIAN_AUTO_RESEARCH=true</code> to let <code>find_and_load</code> reach the web after a weak match. Without that setting, the same call stays local and suggests the explicit <code>research_topic</code> tool.</div>

<h2 id="find-and-load">What find_and_load does</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Searches the local index for up to five matches.</li>
  <li>Reads the top result's unblended BM25 score.</li>
  <li>If the score is greater than <code>0.5</code>, returns the selected skill's <strong>full Markdown file</strong>, its section slugs, and up to three other skill names.</li>
  <li>If the score is not strong enough and auto-research is disabled, returns <code>found: false</code> plus a suggestion to call <code>research_topic</code>.</li>
  <li>If enabled, calls the same research handler used by <code>research_topic</code> and returns its sources.</li>
</ol>
<p>The research response still says <code>found: false</code> because no local skill was found. It does not call <code>create_skill</code>, does not write a draft, and does not update the index. Any persistence is a separate client/model decision and a separate tool call.</p>

<h2 id="explicit-tools">Explicit network tools</h2>
<table class="cmd-table"><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Input</th><th>Network behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>research_topic</code></td><td><code>topic</code>; optional <code>urls</code></td><td>Sends a derived query to DuckDuckGo, combines results with supplied URLs, and fetches up to five pages.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fetch_page</code></td><td><code>url</code></td><td>Fetches one public HTTP(S) URL and returns extracted text.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>These tools always use the network when called. Pack browsing and installation are separate network paths described in <a href="/librarian/wiki/community-packs/">Indexed Packs</a>.</p>

<h2 id="search">Search flow</h2>
<p><code>research_topic</code> appends <code>official documentation best practices</code> to the topic and tries DuckDuckGo's HTML endpoint, then its lite endpoint if no links are extracted. Those endpoints and their markup are not an API contract, so search is best-effort.</p>
<p>The parser extracts up to 15 result links. Caller-supplied URLs are appended, exact URL strings are deduplicated, results are ranked by the coarse mapping below, and the top five are fetched in parallel.</p>

<h2 id="source-scoring">The actual 10/5/1 source mapping</h2>
<p>This research path does not inspect the page's author, citations, date, URL path, code density, or factual accuracy. It scores the hostname:</p>
<table class="cmd-table"><thead><tr><th>Numeric score</th><th>Returned label</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>10</td><td><code>official</code></td><td>Exact or subdomain match in a hardcoded set, including examples such as <code>docs.github.com</code>, <code>developer.mozilla.org</code>, <code>nodejs.org</code>, <code>kubernetes.io</code>, <code>owasp.org</code>, <code>rfc-editor.org</code>, and <code>nist.gov</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td><code>established</code></td><td>The hostname string contains <code>github.com</code> or <code>stackoverflow.com</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td><code>community</code></td><td>Every other parseable hostname.</td></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>Usually <code>failed</code> after fetch</td><td>Unparseable URL before fetch; a fetch failure is returned with the <code>failed</code> label.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>The labels are shorthand, not verification.</strong> A page called <code>official</code> may be outdated or wrong, and the score-5 rule is a substring test rather than a strict registrable-domain check. Review returned URLs and text yourself.</div>

<p>This 10/5/1 mapping is separate from the five-tier source-reputation module used by skill-quality weighting. The two should not be read as one unified authority system.</p>

<h2 id="fetching">Fetch boundary</h2>
<p>Research and <code>fetch_page</code> use the guarded fetcher. It allows HTTP(S), resolves the hostname, rejects any private/loopback/link-local/CGNAT/metadata result, pins a vetted address for the request, rechecks redirects, caps redirects at five, and aborts bodies over 5 MiB. The default timeout is 15 seconds.</p>
<p>This means localhost and private intranet documentation are intentionally unavailable through these tools. The topic is sent to DuckDuckGo, and each selected public host receives a request from your machine.</p>

<h2 id="extraction">HTML extraction is intentionally simple</h2>
<p>The server uses regular-expression transformations, not a browser or a readability engine. It:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Removes <code>script</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>nav</code>, <code>footer</code>, <code>header</code>, and <code>aside</code> elements.</li>
  <li>Converts a narrow <code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;</code> form, inline code, <code>h1</code>-<code>h3</code>, list items, paragraph-like closing tags, and line breaks.</li>
  <li>Strips remaining tags, decodes a small entity set, and normalizes whitespace.</li>
</ul>
<p>It may lose navigation context, links, tables, nested markup, or dynamically rendered content. <code>research_topic</code> limits each returned page to 15,000 extracted characters; <code>fetch_page</code> limits its returned text to 50,000 while reporting the original extracted length.</p>

<h2 id="untrusted">Content remains untrusted</h2>
<p>Extracted text is scanned with the content guard and wrapped in visible <code>UNTRUSTED</code> markers. A flagged page is still returned with a stronger warning; the research tool does not block it. In <code>fetch_page</code>, the optional <code>injectionFlagged</code> field is present when the regex guard flags content. The final mapped source objects from <code>research_topic</code> do not expose that boolean, although the warning is embedded in returned content.</p>
<p>The content guard is pattern-based and exempts code blocks. Treat every fetched page as data regardless of the label or whether a warning appears.</p>

<h2 id="rate-limit">Rate limiting</h2>
<p>The configured <code>rateLimit</code> is a sliding limit on all non-lifecycle MCP requests, not a per-domain crawl delay. It does not establish a target-site politeness policy beyond bounding how often the client can invoke server methods.</p>

<h2 id="safe-use">A defensible workflow</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Keep auto-research off until you need it.</li>
  <li>Call <code>research_topic</code> explicitly with a narrow topic and, when useful, specific public URLs.</li>
  <li>Inspect URLs and compare claims rather than trusting the source label.</li>
  <li>Treat returned text as untrusted even when the content guard is quiet.</li>
  <li>Draft and fact-check a skill separately.</li>
  <li>Run <code>validate_skill</code>, then call <code>create_skill</code> only if you want to persist it.</li>
</ol>
